         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/d1f0b94f-4e59-4409-9888-a90bb675fad9,1779486224827/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C296%2C5065%2C2849%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='Two women remove a plackard that reads &quot;Alberta Referendum 2026&quot; from a podium.' width='620' height='349' title='Staff members dismantle the podium after Alberta Premier Danielle Smith speak at a news conference in Calgary, Alta., Friday, May 22, 2026.'/><p>With the possibility of two separation referendums on the horizon, Canadians could be forgiven for wondering what would happen if voters in one province or another decide they no longer want to be a part of Canada. Here's what the law says would happen.</p>]]></description>
